        {
            "number": 3,
            "revision": "e727cc909ba7f0646ce7fa73fed05daeee2a4cfe",
            "parents": [
                "007e73b8e8f889bc6490dccbf02b41abd97cbfee"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/66/83766/3",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Brad Bishop",
                "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                "username": "bradbishop"
            },
            "createdOn": 1758672585,
            "author": {
                "name": "Brad Bishop",
                "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                "username": "bradbishop"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Overall in support of this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 42,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I'd like to see a couple more added here.  If they don't apply to your case, I'm happy to push them as a dependent patchset on this.\n\n- Must be able to store up to a 3kB CPER\n- Must be able to store 10000 CPER records in the BMC.\n- Must be able to produce a correct Redfish LogEntry, with MessageId in the Platform registry (per Redfish).\n- Must allow polling all CPER stored records within 5 seconds.\n- SHOULD allow reading a CPER in a human readable JSON format through Redfish (format tbd, but this is a should)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 42,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "> Must be able to store up to a 3kB CPER\n> Must be able to store 10000 CPER records in the BMC.\n> Must allow polling all CPER stored records within 5 seconds.\n\nSure.  Is there something behind these numbers?  I think it would be good to document that.\n\n> Must be able to produce a correct Redfish LogEntry, with MessageId in the Platform registry (per Redfish).\n\nThe words I see are:\n\n> The MessageId , event data, or OEM-specific information. This property\n> decodes from the entry type. If the entry type is Event , this property \n> contains a Redfish Specification-defined MessageId . If the entry type is SEL,\n> this property contains the Event Data. Otherwise, this property contains \n> OEM-specific information.\n\nThe entry type is CPER, so this field contains OEM specific information.  I wasn't planning tackling anything along those lines, but I suspect I am missing something.  What am I missing?\n\n> SHOULD allow reading a CPER in a human readable JSON format through Redfish (format tbd, but this is a should).\n\nI was going to follow up with another patch when we want to start parsing."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 46,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "John Chung",
                        "email": "john.chung@arm.com",
                        "username": "gtossk2"
                    },
                    "message": "Do we need a new daemon to host the D-Bus service?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 46,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes.  In my mind D-Bus service == daemon but I am happy to change the wording to make this more clear."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 52,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Should we use the existing DeleteAll interface?  Is there a use case for deleting a specific range of entries rather than just the whole store?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 52,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "I added this because I thought it would be easy to implement and allow bmcweb to remove a bunch of them with one method call.  The use case I pictured was a webui with a bunch of checkboxes next to logs, but I don't have any plans to implement something like that.  It would be trivial to add this interface later so I'll remove it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 52,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "FWIW, the \"delete a single entry\" use case is something I would want to explicitly disable.  If this is removed, I can just resolve this as \"maybe in the future\"\n\n\ud83d\udc4d"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 57,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "John Chung",
                        "email": "john.chung@arm.com",
                        "username": "gtossk2"
                    },
                    "message": "Will we store the CPER data somewhere?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 57,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ravi Teja",
                        "email": "raviteja28031990@gmail.com",
                        "username": "raviteja-b"
                    },
                    "message": "this data need to be persisted across bmc reboots?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 57,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "> Will we store the CPER data somewhere?\n\nYes, I was going to store it in the filesystem somewhere (probably a subdir in /var/lib/).\n\n> this data need to be persisted across bmc reboots?\n\nYes.  I tried to capture this in the requirement:\n\n\"A BMC should be able to receive and store from a CPU, and subsequently recall\n  CPERs.\""
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 67,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Should we set this API up more like post codes, where there's a single method that allows indexing in?  Posting a dbus-object per entry gets performance-critical on startup if you have a lot of messages.\n\nAlso, Redfish has to read a bunch of these to correctly publish the LogService, so minimizing the read size is important."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 67,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": ">  Posting a dbus-object per entry gets performance-critical on startup if you have a lot of messages.\n\nI don't think I'm opposed to changing the semantics as you've suggested but I wouldn't expect any messages on startup other than NameOwnerChanged, irrespective of how many logs there are in the store.  This really is a fundamental misunderstanding (I might be the one that is confused) that we need to align on as a project."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 67,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "If you have a dbus path per entry, you're right, I think we closed on the \"lots of messages at startup\" thing, but you do end up building a gigantic tree that the mapper has to scan.  If that tree has N thousand entries, that has been slow in the past."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 67,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "> you do end up building a gigantic tree that the mapper has to scan\n\n(Not sure why) I didn't think of that.  Will switch to a method based interface."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 67,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "> If that tree has N thousand entries, that has been slow in the past.\n\nHonestly I don't understand why this is slow either.  But in any case, not doing it at all is certainly better."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 67,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "to do an introspect with 1000 entries, you need to build an XML file and send it with 1000 * (rows per object).  Then you have to introspect each object."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 67,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "or just call GetManagedObjects once? ..."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 73,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Is there a use case for this?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 73,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "I was planning to implement this in stages, with the first being just raw logs with no parsing.  I don't remember but think I thought this was an optimization that avoided transferring the entire log.  If we need the entire log even when there is no parsing being done...yeah this is pointless."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 73,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I assumed parsing the whole log (which has a max of 4kB i think?) wasn't that expensive."
                },
                {
                    "file": "designs/cper-repository.md",
                    "line": 73,
                    "reviewer": {
